window.AUTSPANEL_SEARCH=[{"t":"The free cPanel alternative  built for modern apps","p":"AutsPanel · Free, open-source (MIT) hosting control panel for Ubuntu & Debian VPS. Deploy Node.js, Next.js, React, Laravel, PHP & WordPress from Git with SSL and backups.","u":"index.html","k":"","top":true},{"t":"Everything you need to run websites on a VPS","p":"AutsPanel · Git push-to-deploy with rollback, Node.js, Next.js, React, Laravel, PHP, WordPress, free SSL, databases, backups and a firewall — all free in AutsPanel.","u":"features.html","k":"","top":true},{"t":"AutsPanel vs cPanel, Plesk, aaPanel & other control panels","p":"Compare · Compare AutsPanel with cPanel, Plesk, aaPanel, CyberPanel, HestiaCP and CloudPanel: price model, license, web server, Git deployments, Node.js and PHP support.","u":"compare/index.html","k":"","top":true},{"t":"The free, open-source cPanel alternative","p":"Compare · Looking for a free cPanel alternative? AutsPanel is an MIT-licensed VPS control panel with Git deploys, Node.js, PHP, WordPress, SSL and backups — no license fees.","u":"compare/cpanel-alternative.html","k":"","top":true},{"t":"An aaPanel alternative with nothing behind a Pro paywall","p":"Compare · A free, MIT-licensed aaPanel alternative: Git push-to-deploy with rollback, Node.js, Next.js, React, Laravel, PHP, WordPress, SSL and backups — no Pro tier.","u":"compare/aapanel-alternative.html","k":"","top":true},{"t":"A free Plesk alternative for Linux servers","p":"Compare · A free Plesk alternative for Linux VPS: MIT-licensed, unlimited domains, Git deploys with rollback, Node.js, PHP, WordPress, SSL, databases and backups.","u":"compare/plesk-alternative.html","k":"","top":true},{"t":"AutsPanel documentation","p":"Docs · Learn how to install AutsPanel, host websites and apps, deploy from Git, issue SSL, manage databases, PHP, backups and security on an Ubuntu or Debian VPS.","u":"docs/index.html","k":"","top":true},{"t":"What AutsPanel manages","p":"AutsPanel documentation","u":"docs/index.html#what","k":"Everything on the server is organised around websites. A website is one site or app with its domains, code, deployments, .env, app process, PHP version, database, backups and logs. Around that, AutsPanel manages the server itself: Hosting: SSL certificates, PHP versions and Composer, MariaDB/MySQL databases, email hosting, a file manager and a web terminal. Automation: scheduled backups to Google "},{"t":"Requirements at a glance","p":"AutsPanel documentation","u":"docs/index.html#requirements","k":"MinimumRecommendedOperating systemUbuntu 20.04 LTS or Debian 11Ubuntu 24.04 LTSMemory1 GB RAM2 GB+ (Node.js builds, email)Accessroot or sudo over SSHA domain for the panel itself"},{"t":"Getting help","p":"AutsPanel documentation","u":"docs/index.html#help","k":"Found a bug or missing feature? Open an issue on GitHub. For anything else, email tm.aashish1@gmail.com. Last updated 2026-10-19Edit this page on GitHub Next →Installation AutsPanel The free, open-source hosting control panel for Ubuntu & Debian VPS. Deploy apps from Git with SSL, databases, backups and a firewall. Product Features Installation Documentation How it works REST API Compare cPanel al"},{"t":"Install AutsPanel on Ubuntu or Debian","p":"Docs · Install the free AutsPanel control panel on Ubuntu 20.04–24.04 or Debian in about five minutes: clone, set the admin login, run setup.sh, then add HTTPS.","u":"docs/installation.html","k":"","top":true},{"t":"Requirements","p":"Install AutsPanel on Ubuntu or Debian","u":"docs/installation.html#requirements","k":"A VPS with Ubuntu 20.04, 22.04 or 24.04 LTS, or Debian — from any provider (Hetzner, Contabo, DigitalOcean, Linode, Vultr, AWS…). Root access over SSH. AutsPanel runs as root because it manages nginx, PHP, databases and the firewall. At least 1 GB RAM. Use 2 GB or more if you build Node.js apps on the server or host email. Port 9090 reachable for the first login (and 80/443 for websites). If your "},{"t":"1. Install","p":"Install AutsPanel on Ubuntu or Debian","u":"docs/installation.html#install","k":"Connect to the server and clone the repository to /opt/backup-vps: ssh root@YOUR_SERVER_IP git clone https://github.com/auts-tech/backup-vps.git /opt/backup-vps cd /opt/backup-vps cp .env.example .env nano .env In .env, set the admin login and a random session secret: ADMIN_EMAIL=you@example.com ADMIN_PASSWORD=choose-a-long-password PORT=9090 HOST=0.0.0.0 # openssl rand -hex 32 SESSION_SECRET=past"},{"t":"2. Log in","p":"Install AutsPanel on Ubuntu or Debian","u":"docs/installation.html#login","k":"Open http://YOUR_SERVER_IP:9090 and sign in with the email and password from .env. After 10 failed attempts from one IP, logins are blocked for 15 minutes. YOUR_SERVER_IP:9090 If you change .env later, restart the panel with systemctl restart vps-backup-manager."},{"t":"3. Put the panel behind HTTPS","p":"Install AutsPanel on Ubuntu or Debian","u":"docs/installation.html#https","k":"The panel includes a root terminal and file manager, so it should not stay on plain HTTP. Use the panel itself to give it a domain and a certificate: Create a DNS A record such as panel.example.com pointing to the server. Go to SSL / TLS, enter your email and click Install certbot. Go to Websites → New Website → Reverse proxy, enter port 9090 and the domain panel.example.com, tick Enable HTTPS and"},{"t":"4. Turn on the firewall","p":"Install AutsPanel on Ubuntu or Debian","u":"docs/installation.html#firewall","k":"Open Security → Firewall and click Turn on. AutsPanel first allows SSH and the way you currently reach the panel (port 9090, or 80/443 when it is behind the proxy), so you can't lock yourself out. Add rules for anything else you need, such as mail ports."},{"t":"Updating","p":"Install AutsPanel on Ubuntu or Debian","u":"docs/installation.html#update","k":"When a new version is available the panel shows an Update available notice with the list of changes. Update now fast-forwards the code, keeps your settings and websites, installs changed dependencies, checks every server file and rolls back automatically if anything fails, then restarts. From SSH you can do the same: cd /opt/backup-vps git pull sudo bash setup.sh"},{"t":"Service commands","p":"Install AutsPanel on Ubuntu or Debian","u":"docs/installation.html#service","k":"TaskCommandStatussystemctl status vps-backup-managerRestartsystemctl restart vps-backup-managerPanel logsjournalctl -u vps-backup-manager -fJob and backup logtail -f /var/log/vps-backup-manager.logScheduled task outputtail -f /var/log/vps-panel-cron.log"},{"t":"Frequently asked questions","p":"Install AutsPanel on Ubuntu or Debian","u":"docs/installation.html#faq","k":"How long does the installation take?About five minutes on a typical VPS. Most of the time is spent installing Node.js and system packages.Can I install AutsPanel on a server that already has websites?Yes, if they use nginx. Existing nginx sites appear under “Other nginx sites” on the Websites page and can be managed with one click. Don't install it next to another control panel.Which port does Aut"},{"t":"How AutsPanel works","p":"Docs · How AutsPanel works: one Node.js service managing nginx, PM2, PHP-FPM, MariaDB, certbot, UFW, cron and rclone on your VPS, with jobs and a website model.","u":"docs/how-it-works.html","k":"","top":true},{"t":"Overview","p":"How AutsPanel works","u":"docs/how-it-works.html#overview","k":"Browser ──HTTPS──▶ nginx ──▶ AutsPanel (Node.js, port 9090) │ ┌───────────┬───────┼─────────┬──────────┬─────────┐ ▼ ▼ ▼ ▼ ▼ ▼ nginx PM2 PHP-FPM MariaDB certbot UFW · cron (sites) (apps) (5.6–8.x) (databases) (SSL) rclone (backups) Dashboard: a single-page app in plain JavaScript, served by the panel itself. API: an Express REST API under /api/v1. The dashboard uses exactly the same API you can ca"},{"t":"The website model","p":"How AutsPanel works","u":"docs/how-it-works.html#websites","k":"A website record ties together everything that belongs to one site or app: PartWhat AutsPanel does with itDomainsAn nginx server block (static, single-page app, PHP, Laravel, WordPress or reverse proxy) and a Let's Encrypt certificate.CodeA folder, by default /var/www/<name>, optionally a Git repository with a branch.DeploymentsA deploy script, a history with logs, webhooks and rollback.RuntimeA P"},{"t":"Jobs and live logs","p":"How AutsPanel works","u":"docs/how-it-works.html#jobs","k":"Anything that takes more than a moment — creating a website, deploying, installing PHP, issuing a certificate, a backup or restore — runs as a job. A job has a list of steps (for example Clone → Install → Build → Start → Health check → nginx → HTTPS), each with its own status, and streams its output to the browser. You can close the page; the job keeps running. If a setup step fails, fix the cause"},{"t":"How a request reaches your site","p":"How AutsPanel works","u":"docs/how-it-works.html#requests","k":"nginx receives the request on port 80/443 and picks the server block by domain. Static and single-page sites are served from the web root (with an index.html fallback for client-side routes). PHP, Laravel and WordPress sites are passed to the site's PHP-FPM socket. Node.js, Next.js and Python apps are proxied to 127.0.0.1:<port>, where PM2 keeps the app running. The app receives its port in the PO"},{"t":"Where things are stored","p":"How AutsPanel works","u":"docs/how-it-works.html#files","k":"PathContents/opt/backup-vpsThe panel's code and .env (admin login)./opt/backup-vps/server/data/config.json (websites and settings), deployment history and logs, PM2 process files. Credentials files are mode 0600./var/www/<name>Website folders (default)./etc/nginx/sites-available/nginx server blocks, one per website./var/log/vps-backup-manager.logJobs, deployments and backups."},{"t":"Safety rules","p":"How AutsPanel works","u":"docs/how-it-works.html#safety","k":"Every nginx change is checked with nginx -t; if the check fails, the previous configuration is restored. Paths are validated: a website can't be placed in or delete a system folder. The firewall always keeps SSH open, and refuses rules that would block it. Configuration is written atomically, so a crash never leaves a half-written settings file. Panel updates are syntax-checked and rolled back aut"},{"t":"Websites","p":"Docs · Create websites in AutsPanel: deploy Node.js, Next.js, React, Laravel, PHP, Python and static sites from Git, install WordPress, or reverse-proxy any port.","u":"docs/websites.html","k":"","top":true},{"t":"Create a website","p":"Websites","u":"docs/websites.html#create","k":"Click New Website in the top bar and choose how the site starts: Start fromUse it forDeploy from GitNode.js, Next.js, React/Vite/Vue, Laravel, PHP, Python or static sites in a GitHub, GitLab, Gitea or Bitbucket repository.WordPressA new WordPress site with its own database and admin account.Empty websiteHTML or PHP files you upload yourself, for example from another host.Reverse proxyAnything alre"},{"t":"Supported stacks","p":"Websites","u":"docs/websites.html#stacks","k":"StackHow it runsDefault deploy scriptNode.jsPM2 runs npm start (or node index.js) behind an nginx proxynpm ci (or yarn/pnpm), npm run build if presentNext.jsPM2 runs npm start on the site's portinstall, npm run buildReact / Vite / VueStatic files from dist or build with an index.html fallbackinstall, npm run buildLaravelPHP-FPM, web root publiccomposer install --no-dev, migrations (with a database"},{"t":"The website page","p":"Websites","u":"docs/websites.html#page","k":"Open a website from the list to manage it. The tabs are: Overview — status, last deployment, runtime, disk and database size, and a setup checklist. Deployments — deploy, history with logs, rollback and push-to-deploy. See Git deployments. Domains & SSL — domains, DNS status, HTTPS, online/offline, web root, upload limit and the nginx config. See Domains & SSL. Environment — the .env editor. App —"},{"t":"Environment variables","p":"Websites","u":"docs/websites.html#env","k":"The Environment tab edits the .env file in the site folder. Values that look like secrets are hidden until you reveal them, comments and order are kept, and keys missing compared to .env.example are listed. Use Save & restart for Node.js apps or Save & cache config for Laravel. panel.example.com"},{"t":"Apps and processes","p":"Websites","u":"docs/websites.html#app","k":"Node.js, Next.js and Python apps run under PM2. The App tab shows status, uptime, CPU, memory and restarts, the output and error logs, and lets you change the start command and port. When the port changes, the nginx proxy follows automatically. Apps restart if they crash and start again after a reboot. panel.example.com"},{"t":"Import existing nginx sites","p":"Websites","u":"docs/websites.html#import","k":"nginx sites that were created outside AutsPanel are listed under Other nginx sites on the Websites page. Click Manage as website to turn one into a website. AutsPanel reads its domains, root and type, and links a local database if it finds one in the site's .env or wp-config.php."},{"t":"Delete a website","p":"Websites","u":"docs/websites.html#delete","k":"Settings → Delete website removes the website from the panel, stops its app and removes its scheduled jobs. Tick what else should go: the nginx site and its certificate, all files in the folder, and the database with its user. You confirm by typing the website's name."},{"t":"Frequently asked questions","p":"Websites","u":"docs/websites.html#faq","k":"Can I change the folder or port that AutsPanel suggests?Yes. The suggestions are only defaults. You can change the folder, port, web root, branch, build and start commands before creating the website, and most of them later on the Settings tab.How do I host a Next.js app?Create a website from your Git repository. AutsPanel detects Next.js, runs the install and build, starts it with PM2 on a free p"},{"t":"Git deployments","p":"Docs · Deploy from Git with AutsPanel: push-to-deploy webhooks for GitHub, GitLab, Gitea and Bitbucket, editable deploy scripts, history and one-click rollback.","u":"docs/deployments.html","k":"","top":true},{"t":"What a deployment does","p":"Git deployments","u":"docs/deployments.html#deploy","k":"Pull — fetches the branch and fast-forwards to the newest commit. Composer — for PHP sites with a composer.json; Composer is installed if missing. Build — runs the website's deploy script, for example npm ci && npm run build. Permissions — gives PHP sites' writable folders to the web server user. Restart — reloads the PM2 app (PM2 is installed if missing). Health check — waits until the app answer"},{"t":"Deploy on push","p":"Git deployments","u":"docs/deployments.html#webhooks","k":"Open Deployments → Deploy on push and turn it on. AutsPanel shows a webhook URL and a secret: https://panel.example.com/api/v1/hooks/deploy/<website-id> Git hostWhere to add itSecretGitHubRepository → Settings → Webhooks → Add webhook. Content type application/json, event “Just the push event”.Secret field (HMAC X-Hub-Signature-256)GitLabProject → Settings → Webhooks, trigger “Push events”.Secret "},{"t":"The deploy script","p":"Git deployments","u":"docs/deployments.html#script","k":"Each website has an editable deploy script on its Deployments tab. It runs in the website folder with bash, and these variables are available: VariableValueSITE_NAMEThe website's nameSITE_PATHThe website folderBRANCHThe deployed branchPORTThe website's app port (Node.js and Python apps) For PHP sites, php and composer run with the website's own PHP version. A Laravel example: composer install --no"},{"t":"History and rollback","p":"Git deployments","u":"docs/deployments.html#rollback","k":"The history lists every deployment with its trigger (manual, webhook or rollback), commit, author, duration and result; click one to read its full log. Roll back on an earlier successful deployment checks out that commit, builds and restarts. The next push or deploy moves forward again. If files were changed directly on the server, a pull can fail. Tick Discard changes made on the server when depl"},{"t":"Notifications","p":"Git deployments","u":"docs/deployments.html#notifications","k":"Under Settings → Notifications, add a Discord webhook or an email address to be told about successful and failed deployments and backups."},{"t":"Frequently asked questions","p":"Git deployments","u":"docs/deployments.html#faq","k":"Does AutsPanel support GitHub, GitLab and Bitbucket?Yes. Push-to-deploy webhooks work with GitHub, GitLab, Gitea/Forgejo and Bitbucket. Public repositories work over HTTPS; private ones with a deploy key over SSH.Is there zero-downtime deployment?Mostly. The running app keeps serving while dependencies install and the new version builds, and it is restarted only when the build succeeds; a health c"},{"t":"Domains & SSL","p":"Docs · Add domains to a website in AutsPanel, check DNS, issue free Let's Encrypt SSL certificates with automatic renewal, take sites offline and edit nginx settings safely.","u":"docs/domains-ssl.html","k":"","top":true},{"t":"Add domains","p":"Domains & SSL","u":"docs/domains-ssl.html#domains","k":"Enter one domain per line. The first is the primary domain; the others are aliases, such as www.example.com. A domain can belong to only one website. Saving updates the nginx server block and tests it before applying. panel.example.com"},{"t":"Point DNS to the server","p":"Domains & SSL","u":"docs/domains-ssl.html#dns","k":"At your DNS provider, create an A record (and AAAA for IPv6) for each domain with the server's IP address. The tab shows each domain's current DNS status — points here, points elsewhere or no DNS record. If you use Cloudflare's proxy, the check shows Cloudflare's address; issue the certificate with the proxy turned off, or use Cloudflare's “Full (strict)” mode."},{"t":"Issue HTTPS","p":"Domains & SSL","u":"docs/domains-ssl.html#ssl","k":"When the domains point to the server, click Enable HTTPS. AutsPanel runs certbot for all the website's domains, turns on the HTTP → HTTPS redirect, and certbot renews the certificate automatically. The SSL / TLS page lists every certificate on the server with its expiry date. Let's Encrypt limits how many certificates you can request for the same domain per week. If issuing fails, fix the DNS firs"},{"t":"Web server settings","p":"Domains & SSL","u":"docs/domains-ssl.html#settings","k":"Online / offline — take a site offline without deleting it. Web root — the folder nginx serves, for example public or dist. Upload limit — nginx's client_max_body_size, for large uploads."},{"t":"Edit the nginx config","p":"Domains & SSL","u":"docs/domains-ssl.html#nginx","k":"For advanced setups, open Edit nginx config. The editor checks your change with nginx -t and restores the previous file if the check fails, so a typo never takes your other sites down. Last updated 2026-10-19Edit this page on GitHub ← PreviousGit deployments Next →Databases On this pageAdd domainsPoint DNS to the serverIssue HTTPSWeb server settingsEdit the nginx config AutsPanel The free, open-so"},{"t":"Databases","p":"Docs · Manage MariaDB and MySQL in AutsPanel: create databases and users, browse tables, run SQL, import and export dumps, and link existing databases to websites.","u":"docs/databases.html","k":"","top":true},{"t":"Install or connect MariaDB","p":"Databases","u":"docs/databases.html#install","k":"On the Databases page, click Install MariaDB if no database server is installed. If MySQL or MariaDB is already there, click Connect automatically (it uses the root socket login on Ubuntu/Debian) or enter an admin user and password."},{"t":"Databases, users and privileges","p":"Databases","u":"docs/databases.html#manage","k":"Create databases and users, change passwords, and grant or revoke privileges. Browse tables and rows, and run SQL in the console. The Website column shows which website uses each database, and Link to website connects one."},{"t":"A website's database","p":"Databases","u":"docs/databases.html#website","k":"When you create a Laravel or WordPress website with Create a database ticked, AutsPanel creates a database and a user with a random password and writes them to .env (DB_DATABASE, DB_USERNAME, DB_PASSWORD) or wp-config.php. You can also create one later on the website's Database tab."},{"t":"Link an existing database","p":"Databases","u":"docs/databases.html#existing","k":"Already have a database — for example one you imported from another host? Open the website's Database tab. AutsPanel reads the site's configuration and shows the database it finds: .env: DATABASE_URL (mysql://…) or DB_DATABASE/DB_NAME with DB_USERNAME and DB_PASSWORD; wp-config.php: DB_NAME, DB_USER and DB_PASSWORD. If that database is on this server, click Link to connect it. You can also choose "},{"t":"Import and export","p":"Databases","u":"docs/databases.html#import","k":"Export any database as a compressed .sql.gz dump, or import a .sql / .sql.gz file — for example a dump from cPanel's phpMyAdmin. For very large dumps, upload the file with the File manager and import it from the Terminal: gunzip < /root/site.sql.gz | mysql site_db Last updated 2026-10-19Edit this page on GitHub ← PreviousDomains & SSL Next →PHP & Composer On this pageInstall or connect MariaDBData"},{"t":"PHP & Composer","p":"Docs · Run PHP 5.6 to 8.x side by side with AutsPanel: choose a PHP-FPM version per website, manage extensions and php.ini, and run Composer with any version.","u":"docs/php-composer.html","k":"","top":true},{"t":"Install PHP versions","p":"PHP & Composer","u":"docs/php-composer.html#versions","k":"On the PHP & Composer page, click Add repository once. It adds the ondrej/php repository on Ubuntu or packages.sury.org on Debian. Then install any version from 5.6 to 8.x with the common extensions. Several versions run side by side, each with its own FPM service. panel.example.com"},{"t":"Choose a website's PHP version","p":"PHP & Composer","u":"docs/php-composer.html#site","k":"New PHP, Laravel and WordPress websites use the newest installed version — or the one matching the php requirement in composer.json. Switch at any time on the website's PHP tab; nginx is updated and tested automatically."},{"t":"Extensions and php.ini","p":"PHP & Composer","u":"docs/php-composer.html#ini","k":"Enable or disable extensions per version and edit common php.ini settings such as memory_limit, upload_max_filesize, post_max_size and max_execution_time. You can also choose the default php command for the terminal."},{"t":"Composer","p":"PHP & Composer","u":"docs/php-composer.html#composer","k":"The Composer section installs Composer (with its checksum verified) and keeps it updated. Run install, update, require, remove, dump-autoload and more in any project folder, with the PHP version you choose and live output. Deployments run composer install automatically for PHP sites."},{"t":"Laravel scheduler","p":"PHP & Composer","u":"docs/php-composer.html#laravel","k":"Turn on Laravel scheduler on a Laravel website's PHP tab. AutsPanel adds the cron entry that runs php artisan schedule:run every minute with the site's PHP version."},{"t":"Frequently asked questions","p":"PHP & Composer","u":"docs/php-composer.html#faq","k":"Which PHP versions are supported?Any version the ondrej/php (Ubuntu) or sury.org (Debian) repositories provide for your release, typically PHP 5.6 up to the latest 8.x.My PHP site shows 502 Bad Gateway. What now?The site's PHP-FPM service is not running. Start it on the PHP & Composer page or switch the site to an installed version on its PHP tab. Last updated 2026-10-19Edit this page on GitHub ← "},{"t":"Backups & restore","p":"Docs · Back up websites (database, files and .env) to Google Drive, Amazon S3 or SFTP on a schedule with AutsPanel, restore with a live log, and take full VPS snapshots.","u":"docs/backups.html","k":"","top":true},{"t":"What is backed up","p":"Backups & restore","u":"docs/backups.html#what","k":"The website's database as a compressed dump. The website folder, skipping node_modules (it is rebuilt on deploy). The .env file. Run Back up now on a website's Backups tab, or Backup All Websites on the Backups page. A VPS snapshot archives the whole server except system folders such as /proc, /sys, /tmp and caches — useful for disaster recovery, but large."},{"t":"Backup destinations","p":"Backups & restore","u":"docs/backups.html#targets","k":"DestinationSetupGoogle DriveSettings → Google Drive. Use an OAuth token (rclone.conf) for a personal Drive, or a service account with a Google Workspace shared drive.S3-compatibleAmazon S3, Backblaze B2, Wasabi, Cloudflare R2, MinIO… — bucket, region, keys and endpoint.SFTPAny server with SSH: host, user, password or key, and a folder. Google service accounts have no storage of their own on person"},{"t":"Schedules and retention","p":"Backups & restore","u":"docs/backups.html#schedule","k":"Set a schedule for each website on its Backups tab, or use the presets on the Cron page (backup all websites, VPS snapshot, cleanup). Settings → Retention policy (30 days by default) decides when the cleanup task deletes old backups from remote storage. Every scheduled run writes to /var/log/vps-panel-cron.log."},{"t":"Restore a website","p":"Backups & restore","u":"docs/backups.html#restore","k":"On the website's Backups tab, click Restore next to a backup and choose what to restore: the database, the files and/or .env. A job with a live log downloads the backup, imports the database and extracts the files back into the website folder. Restart the app from the App tab afterwards. Test a restore once after setting up backups. A backup you have never restored is only a hope. Last updated 202"},{"t":"Security","p":"Docs · Secure an AutsPanel server: HTTPS for the panel, UFW rules that never lock you out, SSH and deploy keys, login rate limits and signed deploy webhooks.","u":"docs/security.html","k":"","top":true},{"t":"Secure the panel","p":"Security","u":"docs/security.html#panel","k":"Use a long, unique ADMIN_PASSWORD and a random SESSION_SECRET in .env. Serve the panel over HTTPS on a domain and set HOST=127.0.0.1 so port 9090 is closed (see Installation). Logins are rate-limited: 10 failures per IP in 15 minutes block further attempts. Sessions expire after 24 hours."},{"t":"Firewall","p":"Security","u":"docs/security.html#firewall","k":"Security → Firewall manages UFW. When you click Turn on, AutsPanel first allows SSH (on your real SSH port) and the way you reach the panel. Then add rules: Quick rules for Web (HTTP + HTTPS) and Email (SMTP, IMAP). Allow, deny, reject or limit a port or range over TCP, UDP or both, optionally only from an IP or network. Block IP — the rule is placed first so it takes effect immediately. Rules tha"},{"t":"SSH keys","p":"Security","u":"docs/security.html#ssh","k":"Security → SSH Keys lists the keys that can log in as root, with fingerprints; add or remove keys there. The same page generates the server's deploy key (ed25519) for private Git repositories with a Test the connection button for GitHub, GitLab and Bitbucket. Once your SSH key works, consider turning off password logins in /etc/ssh/sshd_config (PasswordAuthentication no)."},{"t":"Services and ports","p":"Security","u":"docs/security.html#services","k":"Services lists systemd services such as nginx, MariaDB, PHP-FPM, cron and Docker: start, stop, restart, enable at boot and read their journal. Ports & Processes shows every listening port, whether it is public, whether the firewall allows it, and which website, app or service it belongs to."},{"t":"Security model","p":"Security","u":"docs/security.html#model","k":"All API routes under /api/v1 require a session token or the API key. The only unauthenticated endpoint is the per-website deploy webhook. It is off until you enable it and checks the website's own secret (HMAC signature or GitLab token). Names, domains, paths, branches, ports and IP addresses are validated before use, and passed to tools as separate arguments wherever possible. Database and mail c"},{"t":"REST API","p":"Docs · Use the AutsPanel REST API to automate websites, deployments, backups and jobs: authentication with a session token or API key, endpoints and examples with curl.","u":"docs/api.html","k":"","top":true},{"t":"Authentication","p":"REST API","u":"docs/api.html#auth","k":"Use either a session token from the login endpoint (valid for 24 hours) or the API key from Settings → Admin API Key: # Session token curl -s -X POST https://panel.example.com/api/v1/auth/login \\ -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \\ -d '{\"email\":\"you@example.com\",\"password\":\"…\"}' # → {\"success\":true,\"token\":\"…\"} curl -s https://panel.example.com/api/v1/sites -H 'Authorization: Bearer TOKEN' # API"},{"t":"Websites","p":"REST API","u":"docs/api.html#sites","k":"Method & pathDescriptionGET /sitesAll websites with status, plus unmanaged nginx sitesGET /sites/suggest?name=&domain=&repo=&type=Suggested name, folder, free port and PHP versionPOST /sites/inspect{ gitUrl, branch } → branches, detected stack, commands, PHP versionPOST /sitesCreate a website → { site, jobId }GET /sites/:idDetails, health, runtime and databasePUT /sites/:idUpdate settings (branch,"},{"t":"Deployments","p":"REST API","u":"docs/api.html#deploy","k":"Method & pathDescriptionPOST /sites/:id/deployDeploy now → { jobId, deployId }; 409 if one is runningPOST /sites/:id/rollback{ commit } deploy an earlier commitGET /sites/:id/deploymentsHistory, newest firstGET /sites/:id/deployments/:deployId/logLog of one deploymentGET /sites/:id/git/pendingCommits not deployed yetPOST /sites/:id/webhook{ enabled, rotate } push-to-deployPOST /hooks/deploy/:idThe"},{"t":"Jobs","p":"REST API","u":"docs/api.html#jobs","k":"Long operations return a jobId. Poll it for status, steps and new output: Method & pathDescriptionGET /jobsRecent jobsGET /jobs/:id?offset=NStatus, steps and output after character NPOST /jobs/:id/cancelStop a running job"},{"t":"Other endpoints","p":"REST API","u":"docs/api.html#other","k":"The other modules follow the same pattern: /databases, /php, /backups, /cron, /firewall, /ssh, /nginx, /files, /mail, /monitor, /system and /settings. Each route file in server/routes documents its endpoints in comments. Last updated 2026-10-19Edit this page on GitHub ← PreviousSecurity Next →Troubleshooting On this pageAuthenticationWebsitesDeploymentsJobsOther endpoints AutsPanel The free, open-"},{"t":"Troubleshooting","p":"Docs · Fix common AutsPanel problems: dashboard not loading, login errors, failed deployments, 502 Bad Gateway, SSL errors, backup errors and cron jobs.","u":"docs/troubleshooting.html","k":"","top":true},{"t":"The dashboard doesn't load","p":"Troubleshooting","u":"docs/troubleshooting.html#dashboard","k":"systemctl status vps-backup-manager ss -tulpn | grep 9090 ufw status journalctl -u vps-backup-manager -n 100 Also check your provider's cloud firewall (for example Contabo, Hetzner or AWS security groups) allows the port."},{"t":"Can't log in","p":"Troubleshooting","u":"docs/troubleshooting.html#login","k":"The login comes from ADMIN_EMAIL and ADMIN_PASSWORD in /opt/backup-vps/.env. Restart the service after changing it: systemctl restart vps-backup-manager. “Too many failed logins” clears itself after 15 minutes."},{"t":"A deployment failed","p":"Troubleshooting","u":"docs/troubleshooting.html#deploy","k":"Open the deployment in the history and read the step that failed — the log shows each command as it runs. Pull failed: the repository can't be reached (add a deploy key for private repositories), or files were changed on the server — use Discard server changes. Build failed: run the same command in the Terminal inside the site folder. Out of memory? Add swap or a bigger plan. Health check failed: "},{"t":"502 Bad Gateway","p":"Troubleshooting","u":"docs/troubleshooting.html#502","k":"Node.js / Python: the app is stopped or crashed — check the App tab. PHP: the site's PHP-FPM version isn't running — start it on the PHP page or switch versions."},{"t":"HTTPS can't be issued","p":"Troubleshooting","u":"docs/troubleshooting.html#ssl","k":"Every domain on the website must point to this server — check the DNS status on the Domains & SSL tab. Remove domains that don't. Port 80 must be open to the internet for the HTTP challenge. Behind Cloudflare's proxy? Turn the proxy off while issuing, or use an origin certificate."},{"t":"Backups fail","p":"Troubleshooting","u":"docs/troubleshooting.html#backups","k":"Google Drive 403 storageQuotaExceeded: service accounts can't store files on personal drives; use an OAuth token or a shared drive. Use Test connection in Settings for each destination to see rclone's output."},{"t":"Cron jobs don't run","p":"Troubleshooting","u":"docs/troubleshooting.html#cron","k":"The Cron page warns if the cron service is stopped — click Start cron. Check /var/log/vps-panel-cron.log and use Run to test a job. Custom commands run with a minimal PATH: use absolute paths such as /usr/bin/php8.3 and write % as \\%."},{"t":"Terminal in basic mode","p":"Troubleshooting","u":"docs/troubleshooting.html#terminal","k":"The node-pty module didn't compile. Install build tools and reinstall: apt install -y build-essential python3 cd /opt/backup-vps/server && npm install systemctl restart vps-backup-manager"},{"t":"Couldn't check for updates","p":"Troubleshooting","u":"docs/troubleshooting.html#updates","k":"The server can't reach the Git remote — check its network, or add a deploy key if your copy comes from a private fork. “Files were edited on the server” means tracked files were changed by hand: run git status in /opt/backup-vps and commit or revert them. Still stuck? Open an issue with the job log, or email tm.aashish1@gmail.com. Last updated 2026-10-19Edit this page on GitHub ← PreviousREST API "},{"t":"Free hosting software, made in the open","p":"AutsPanel · AutsPanel is a free, MIT-licensed VPS control panel developed by Aashish Timalsina and published by auts-tech. Contact, contributing and project background.","u":"about.html","k":"","top":true}];
